n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n2,\\,6,\\,18,\\,54,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n}\\cdot {3}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc2548584afdb3016c6caf43bf436c68ea1ae5b5f9e2fdb262895e441a976cbc"} {"question" "q-183789" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183789" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\cdot {2}^{n}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{n}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {2}^{n}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}"]] "category" "