pture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183328" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Describe the set $S={…,\\frac{1}{4},1,4,16,64,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=4$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,1,\\,4,\\,16,\\,64,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4 \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=4,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}\\cdot {4}^{n} \\\\  & ={4}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={{4}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/state.json" "file_sha256" "792966511c878b86a039ac7de776761cc4b5ff959d6c68585ce77cf462836283"} {"question" "q-183779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderat